fix(psci): correct PSCI_FEATURES semantics and re-test SYSTEM_RESET2

PSCI_FEATURES returns PSCI_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED (0xffffffff) for a function the
firmware does not implement.  Zero is the normal answer for "supported, no
feature flags".  The previous diagnosis read zero as "not supported" and
therefore recorded SYSTEM_RESET2 as absent, even though SYSTEM_RESET - which
demonstrably resets this board - answers zero as well.  Test against
0xffffffff, the same check Linux makes in drivers/firmware/psci.c, and keep
the raw 32-bit answers so the console prints what the firmware actually said
instead of a re-interpretation of it.

  * PSCI? now prints the negative control FEATURES(0x8400ffff) first, then the
    raw answers for SYSTEM_RESET and SYSTEM_RESET2.  PSCINEG? exposes the
    negative control on its own.  If the control does not return 0xffffffff
    the probe is marked untrustworthy and FASTBOOT2 refuses to run.
  * FASTBOOT2 is gated on the corrected rule plus that control.  It logs the
    old IMEM value, writes the cookie (still overridable with
    kebab_fastboot_cookie), reads it back, issues dsb sy, and only then calls
    SYSTEM_RESET2(reset_type=0, cookie=0).  A return is reported as the full
    64-bit x0 and execution stops there: it never falls through to
    SYSTEM_RESET.  If the cookie was never delivered the old IMEM value is
    restored.
  * The PSCI exports carry their real widths (uint32_t for PSCI_FEATURES,
    uint64_t for SYSTEM_RESET2), so no narrowing or sign interpretation
    happens between the SMC and the log.

The earlier NOT_SUPPORTED conclusion was an artifact of the wrong comparison.
This change re-measures both real FIDs and adds the experiment that follows
from a supported answer.
